    expectedAnswer: "**Playwright Architecture** should cover a critical user journey through the real application boundary while keeping selectors, data, and environment behavior deterministic.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\n\n**Playwright Architecture** should cover a critical user journey through the real application boundary while keeping selectors, data, and environment behavior deterministic.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the observable behavior, inputs, expected result, failure conditions, and cleanup. Use the smallest test level that provides enough confidence.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual version exposes the core logic so it is easy to explain line by line in an interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface JourneyStep { name: string; completed: boolean; }\n\nfunction journeyComplete(steps: readonly JourneyStep[]): boolean {\n  for (let i = 0; i < steps.length; i += 1) {\n    if (!steps[i].completed) return false;\n  }\n\n  return steps.length > 0;\n}\n\nconst complete = journeyComplete([\n  { name: \"login\", completed: true },\n  { name: \"checkout\", completed: true },\n]);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native array/collection helpers when they make the same behavior clearer without hiding the important test idea.\n\n```ts\nconst steps = [\n  { name: \"login\", completed: true },\n  { name: \"checkout\", completed: true },\n];\n\nconst complete =\n  steps.length > 0 &&\n  steps.every((step) => step.completed);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nKeep a small number of high-value user journeys deterministic with stable selectors and controlled data.\n\nStep 6 — Test quality:\nKeep the test deterministic, isolated, diagnostic, and representative. Cover success, boundary, failure, accessibility, and cleanup where relevant.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why this belongs at this test level, what it deliberately does not cover, how flakiness is prevented, and how it fits the wider suite.",
